          </p>

          <p>
            First, thermostat quality. An electronic thermostat that steps down to a low setting holds a room steady without running at full power longer than needed; a heater with no thermostat runs flat out until you switch it off. Second, documented safety: a tip-over switch, overheat protection and a listing mark from a recognized testing laboratory such as UL, Intertek (ETL) or CSA. Third, how the heat arrives, fan-forced or radiant, which decides whether a heater suits a bedroom or a drafty den. Finally, we chose one pick per heater type rather than seven versions of the same ceramic box, and favoured long warranties and available replacement parts.
          </p>
