        <div id="btu-truth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 5,118 BTU Truth About "Energy Efficient" Heaters
          </h2>

          <p>
            One watt of electricity converted to heat equals about 3.412 BTU per hour. A heater drawing 1,500 watts therefore produces about 5,118 BTU per hour, whether it is a ceramic cube, an oil-filled radiator, a mica panel or a wood-cabinet infrared heater. Resistance heating is effectively 100 percent efficient at the point of use: all the electricity becomes heat, because there is nowhere else for it to go. The fan motor and the display lights end up as heat in the room too.
          </p>

          <p>
            So claims of "more heat" from a plug-in heater should make you suspicious. One manufacturer on our list advertises up to 60 percent more heat output, while its own manual rates the heater at 5,200 BTU, which is 5,118 rounded up. Another says its panel converts 100 percent of electricity into heat, which is true of the cheapest heater on the shelf too. The 1,500-watt cap is practical: at 120 volts it draws 12.5 amps, close to the limit of the 15-amp circuits most rooms are wired with.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Watts in equals heat out. No plug-in heater beats the physics.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 1,500-watt heater makes about 5,118 BTU per hour, full stop. What a better heater can do is make less heat when less is needed, and put the heat where you are.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            So what does "energy efficient" mean on a heater box? Done honestly, it means a heater that wastes less of its output. A precise thermostat and a low setting stop it overheating a room you only needed warm. Timers and occupancy sensing stop it heating an empty room. Good air circulation or radiant delivery lets you feel comfortable at a lower setpoint. Those features can save real money. The heating element itself cannot.
          </p>
        </div>

