        <div id="heater-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ceramic, Oil-Filled, Mica, Infrared or Wall: Which Type Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Since every type makes the same heat per watt, the choice comes down to how quickly the heat arrives, how it feels, how much noise you will tolerate and where the heater has to live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceramic fan heaters</h3>

          <p>
            A ceramic heater blows air across a self-regulating ceramic element. It warms you within a minute or two, it is light and cheap, and a good one, like the Vornado AVH2, circulates the warm air instead of blasting a hot spot. The costs are fan noise and dust; Vornado's manual recommends cleaning the outside weekly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil-filled radiators</h3>

          <p>
            An oil-filled radiator heats a sealed reservoir of oil, which warms the fins and the room by radiation and convection. It is silent and keeps giving off heat after it cycles off, but it is slow to take the chill off a cold room and it is heavy. It suits a bedroom you warm before you need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Micathermic panels</h3>

          <p>
            A mica panel uses an element sheathed in thin mica sheets. With no oil to warm, it responds faster than a radiator but stays quiet, slim and often wall-mountable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Infrared, quartz and wall heaters</h3>

          <p>
            Infrared heaters warm the people and objects they face rather than the air first, which is why they feel effective in drafty rooms. Cabinet models like the Dr. Infrared DR-968 add a blower, so they behave much like a quiet fan heater with a radiant component. A hardwired fan-forced wall heater, like the Cadet Com-Pak, is permanent heat for a room that needs it every winter: no cord, nothing to tip, but it needs its own circuit.
          </p>

