        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Size a Space Heater to the Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rule of thumb heater retailers publish, Sylvane's buying guide among them, is about 10 watts of heat per square foot of floor when the heater is the main heat source in a room with ordinary 8-foot ceilings and average insulation. By that rule a 1,500-watt heater covers about 150 square feet as primary heat, a 12-by-12 bedroom with a little to spare. As supplemental heat, topping up a room the furnace already warms, it will stretch further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjust for the room you actually have. Older rooms with single-pane windows, little insulation or an outside wall on two sides need more, and installers commonly use 12 to 15 watts per square foot for drafty spaces. Ceilings higher than 8 feet need more too, because there is more air to warm. A tight, modern room can get by with less. Ignore any BTU figure printed beside a watt rule unless it uses the right conversion; a watt is about 3.4 BTU per hour, not 5.
          </p>

