        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety: The Features and Habits That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Space heaters are one of the few appliances in a house that can start a fire while working exactly as designed. The Consumer Product Safety Commission estimates that portable heaters, including electric space heaters, were involved in an average of 1,600 fires a year from 2020 to 2022, causing an average of 70 deaths and 150 injuries annually. The National Fire Protection Association counts an estimated average of 38,881 home heating equipment fires a year from 2019 to 2023, with 432 civilian deaths, and space heaters and heating stoves were the equipment involved in 29 percent of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three features should be non-negotiable. A listing mark from a nationally recognized testing laboratory, such as UL, Intertek's ETL or CSA, means the design was tested against a safety standard; Massachusetts' Department of Fire Services, among others, tells buyers to look for one. A tip-over switch cuts power if the heater falls. Overheat protection shuts it down if the intake is blocked or it runs too hot. Be particularly careful with no-name heaters from online marketplaces, where a listing mark printed in a photo is not the same as one on the rating label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE THREE-FOOT RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep anything that can burn at least 3 feet away.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">CPSC and the US Fire Administration give the same distance: three feet from drapes, furniture, bedding and anything else that can burn. The heater manuals we read say the same.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Habits matter as much as hardware. CPSC says to turn heaters off when sleeping, and the US Fire Administration says to turn them off when you leave the room or go to bed, so warm a bedroom before bed and let the timer finish the job. Keep pets and children out of the three-foot zone, never run the cord under a rug, and keep working smoke alarms on every level.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outlets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Outlets, Circuits and Why Extension Cords Are Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            CPSC's instruction is blunt: plug electric space heaters directly into a wall outlet, never into an extension cord or power strip. The heater manuals we read say the same; Lasko's adds surge protectors, multi-outlet adapters and cord reels to the banned list. The reason is current. A 1,500-watt heater draws 12.5 amps continuously for hours, and a light-duty extension cord or a cheap power strip was never designed for that load, so it heats up at its connections, which is exactly where fires start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The wall outlet can be the weak link too. An old receptacle with worn contacts grips the plug loosely, the connection heats, and the plug or faceplate gets hot. Dr. Heater's manual tells owners to check the plug and outlet often while the heater runs and to have the outlet replaced if either gets hot. A plug that feels warm is normal; one too hot to hold is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the circuit. A typical bedroom circuit is 15 amps and also feeds lamps, a TV and chargers. If the breaker trips, Vornado's manual suggests running only one heater at once, unplugging other electrical products while it runs, or having an electrician add a circuit. Two heaters in one room belong on two different circuits. A hardwired heater like the Cadet Com-Pak sidesteps all of this on its own circuit; Cadet's manual caps a breaker's load at 80 percent of its rating, so a 1,500-watt, 120-volt unit belongs on a 20-amp circuit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zone-heating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Use a Space Heater to Actually Save Money
          </h2>

          <p>
            A space heater only saves money when it lets you turn something else down. Run one in the living room while the furnace keeps the whole house at 70 degrees, and you have simply added up to 27 cents an hour to your bill. The saving comes from zone heating: keeping the room you are in comfortable while the central thermostat sits lower for the rest of the house. The Department of Energy says turning a thermostat back 7 to 10 degrees for 8 hours a day can save up to 10 percent a year on heating and cooling, and a space heater is what makes that setback livable in the one room you occupy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the door so the warmth stays with you, set the heater to the lowest temperature you are comfortable at, and use the timer. With a smart model like the Lasko AR122, let occupancy sensing switch it off when you walk out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Move the warm air, too. Heat rises and pools at the ceiling, so in winter run a ceiling fan on low in the reverse, clockwise direction to push it gently back down without creating a chilly breeze; our guide to <a href="/ceiling-fan-direction-summer-and-winter/">ceiling fan direction in summer and winter</a> explains the switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the humidity rather than the marketing. Heating air lowers its relative humidity because warm air can hold more moisture, so any heater can make a room feel drier, even the radiators sold as not drying the air. If the room feels dry, that is a humidifier question; if you are fighting condensation on cold windows instead, read <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">whether you need a dehumidifier in winter</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When a Space Heater Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are heating the same room every evening all winter, look at the bill honestly. A heat pump moves heat from outside rather than making it from resistance, and the Department of Energy's Energy Saver guidance puts the reduction in electricity used for heating at roughly half or more compared with electric resistance heat, including plug-in space heaters. If your home already heats with a heat pump, a resistance space heater produces heat less efficiently than the system it supplements, so it only pays in a single room with the rest of the house set well back. For a cold addition or a converted garage, a ductless unit is usually the better long-term buy; see our <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">best mini split heat pumps</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            A garage or workshop is another poor fit. At 5,118 BTU per hour, a plug-in heater barely warms your hands at a bench in a two-car garage, and space heater manuals warn against using them where gasoline, paint or other flammable liquids are stored. A properly sized 240-volt or vented gas unit is the right tool, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-heater/">best garage heaters</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power outages are the third trap. A space heater is one of the hungriest things you can plug into a battery: at 1,500 watts, a 1,000 watt-hour power station would be empty in about 40 minutes even before inverter losses. Our explainer on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">whether a portable power station can run a space heater</a> runs the numbers and covers better ways to stay warm.
          </p>

          <p>
            And never bring a fuel-burning heater made for outdoor or garage use into a living space. Unvented combustion heaters can produce carbon monoxide; an electric heater does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying extra for an "energy saving" heating element</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No element, mica, quartz, ceramic or oil, makes more heat per watt than another; at 1,500 watts they all make about 5,118 BTU per hour. Pay for the features that reduce running time, an electronic thermostat, a low setting, a timer or occupancy sensing, and ignore claims of extra heat output.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into a power strip or extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heater draws 12.5 amps for hours, which overheats light-duty cords and power strips at their connections. CPSC and every manual we read say to plug straight into a wall outlet. If the plug or faceplate gets too hot to hold, stop and have the outlet replaced.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the square footage on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage claims of 500 or 1,000 square feet assume a well-insulated room that is already mostly warm. As primary heat, plan on about 10 watts per square foot, or 150 square feet for a 1,500-watt heater, and more for drafty rooms or high ceilings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it with the central heat unchanged</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A space heater only saves money when the main thermostat goes down while it runs. Heating one room on top of a whole house at 70 degrees just adds up to 27 cents an hour at the current average power price. Close the door and set the house back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Crowding it or leaving it on overnight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bedding, curtains and sofas within three feet of a heater are how most heater fires start. Keep the three-foot zone clear, keep the cord out from under rugs, and follow CPSC's advice to switch heaters off when you sleep; warm the room first and let the timer finish the job.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do energy-efficient space heaters really use less electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not per unit of heat. Every resistance heater converts nearly all its electricity into heat, so a 1,500-watt model makes about 5,118 BTU per hour regardless of the technology. A so-called efficient heater can use less electricity overall only by running less, through a precise thermostat, a low setting, a timer or occupancy sensing that shuts it off in an empty room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a space heater all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the US average residential price of 18.31 cents per kWh that EIA reported for July 2026, a 1,500-watt heater running flat out for 8 hours uses 12 kWh and costs about $2.20. A thermostat model that cycles half the time costs about half that. Use the price on your own bill, since rates vary widely by state.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size space heater do I need for a 12x12 room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12-by-12 room is 144 square feet. At the common rule of thumb of about 10 watts per square foot, it needs roughly 1,440 watts as its main heat source, so a standard 1,500-watt heater is right. A drafty room or one with a ceiling higher than 8 feet may need more than one plug-in heater can deliver.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an oil-filled radiator cheaper to run than a ceramic heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. At the same wattage both make the same heat for the same money. The oil stores heat and releases it slowly, so a radiator is quieter and gives steadier warmth, which may let you set the thermostat a little lower. It also warms a cold room more slowly, so it is not cheaper per hour of use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to leave a space heater on overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                CPSC advises turning heaters off when you sleep, and the US Fire Administration says to switch them off when you leave the room or go to bed. If you heat a bedroom, run it before bed with a timer set to shut it off, keep bedding at least three feet away, and choose a heater with a tip-over switch and overheat protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a space heater into an extension cord or power strip?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. CPSC says to plug space heaters directly into a wall outlet, never an extension cord or power strip, and heater manuals add surge protectors and multi-outlet adapters to that list. A heater's 12.5-amp continuous draw can overheat those devices at their connections. If you need heat where there is no outlet, a hardwired wall heater is the safer fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a space heater cheaper than central heat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when it lets you heat less space. Heating one closed room with a space heater while the central thermostat is set back can cut costs; the Department of Energy says a 7 to 10 degree setback for 8 hours a day can save up to 10 percent a year. If you heat with a heat pump, resistance heat costs more per unit of warmth.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

