        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the heater for its controls and its safety record, not its heating element. Every 1,500-watt model on the market makes the same 5,118 BTU per hour, so the money is in a thermostat that throttles back, a tip-over switch, a real listing mark and a warranty long enough to matter. For most rooms that is the Vornado AVH2; for a silent bedroom, the De'Longhi Dragon; for a room that is cold every winter, a hardwired Cadet.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then use it like a zone heater: close the door, turn the house thermostat down, plug it straight into the wall, and keep three feet clear around it. Do that and a space heater is a cheap, safe way through winter 2026–2027. Skip those steps and it is an expensive one.
          </p>
